# Server modules

Start every file under `server/` with `import "server-only"`. Domain, use cases, repositories, adapters, auth, workers, and audit live here. Routes and components call use cases. They do not contain business rules.

One Firestore transaction per business change writes the business state, the audit row, and outbox items together.

```ts
// Bad: Stripe, Resend, fetch, or Storage inside the callback. It can re-run.
await db.runTransaction(async (tx) => {
  await stripe.checkout.sessions.create({ /* ... */ });
});

// Good: commit state + audit + outbox, then call Stripe with an idempotency key.
await db.runTransaction(async (tx) => {
  tx.set(bookingRef, { checkoutOpId, checkoutPending: true });
  tx.set(auditRef, { action: "checkout.requested" });
  tx.set(outboxRef, { status: "pending" });
});
await stripe.checkout.sessions.create(params, { idempotencyKey: checkoutOpId });
```

Do not take amounts from the client. Money is integer cents, EUR. Stay dates are `YYYY-MM-DD`, Europe/Rome, check-out exclusive. Logs carry correlation and booking ids only. Firebase Admin uses Application Default Credentials. No service-account key files.
